---
/*
  ACHIEVEMENTS STRIP — restores the round-1 stats-row presentation the
  owner explicitly liked (small uppercase intro label, several big
  numbers side by side, each with one short context line, staggered
  reveal) after round 2's single-fact "capability strips" misread the
  direction. Extended, per the round-3 correction, to repeat that exact
  presentation ONCE PER CASE STUDY within one shared section — each
  block gets its own heading + one-line subline, its own 2-4 stats, and
  a closing inline link out to where the full story lives.

  ROUND 5 — owner verdict on round 4's tinted panels: "badly colored
  (AI weak colors)" (too washed-out to read as confident color),
  "numbers seem too big", and "not sitting right" (composition). Fixed
  by moving from round 4's low-alpha tints to solid, theme-invariant
  fills (see the `.strip-panel-<tone>` comment in global.css for the
  full color-distance/contrast math) — a decisive jump in how distinct
  the 4 panels read from each other. Because each panel's fill no
  longer changes by theme, panel text also stopped using the usual
  `text-text-primary`/`text-text-muted` theme tokens (which would flip
  to near-black in light theme and vanish) in favor of fixed
  `text-white`/`text-slate-300` — see TEXT below. Per-tone `link` and
  `bar` classes from round 4 are gone: with the whole panel now
  carrying its tone, a single shared light color for all panel text
  reads correctly against all four fills at once (verified, see
  global.css), so there's no more need for 4 separate tone-matched text
  variants. Stat value size stepped down again (2xl/3xl → xl/2xl, the
  second such step after round 4's 3xl/4xl → 2xl/3xl). Composition:
  panel-to-panel gap widened slightly (gap-4 → gap-5) since bolder
  panels need a touch more breathing room than round 4's faint ones
  did; internal margins (subline→stats, stats→link) opened one notch
  for the same reason.

  Every value must be traceable to a real source (see CLAUDE.md, Content
  honesty) — never invent or round a number to make it look better. The
  one exception is deliberate, owner-requested, and marked inline: see
  the [OWNER NUMBER NEEDED] placeholders wired up in index.astro, never
  in this component. Reception's placeholders are still pending an
  owner decision as of round 5 — untouched here.

  STAT-CELL LOOK — round-1 DNA preserved (`border-s-2 ... ps-4`,
  extrabold value, staggered `--reveal-delay`); the accent bar itself
  is now a plain light divider (`border-white/25`, not a per-tone hue)
  since a colored bar would fight rather than complement a now-solidly
  colored panel — round 4's stat-cell bars were tone-colored precisely
  because the panel fill was too faint to carry color on its own; that
  reasoning no longer applies once the panel itself is the color
  statement. Same visual language (a short vertical accent before each
  number), adjusted per the Task Prompt's explicit allowance to do so
  "unless it fights the new tinted backgrounds."

  GRID SHAPE — must look intentional at both 3 and 4 stats, not like a
  4-column grid with an empty cell. 4 stats: 2 cols mobile, 4 cols sm+.
  3 stats: 1 col mobile (stacked), 3 cols sm+. Never stretch 3 across 4.
  (As of round 5, every strip has exactly 3 stats — fuel dropped its
  4th "1 week" stat per the finalized copy — but the 4-stat branch is
  kept for schema flexibility; nothing currently exercises it.)

  CASCADE-LAYER NOTE: `.strip-panel-<tone>` is defined after `.strip-
  panel` in global.css (same reasoning as `.card-row`/`.card-compact`)
  so its border-color/background win by source order within the same
  compiled utilities layer. `text-white`/`text-slate-300` on the
  heading/value/description/link are plain Tailwind utilities with no
  competing component-class base setting `color` on those elements —
  no cascade risk stacking them directly. The closing link still uses
  `.focus-ring` + `.focus-ring-on-900` (a small, accepted mismatch
  against non-surface-900 fills — only visible on keyboard focus, not
  worth a new per-tone offset system for this round).

  LINK, NOT A STRETCHED CARD: unlike round 2's CapabilityStrips (whole
  row as a single stretched-link target), each block here has real
  content beyond the link (heading, subline, several stats) — a plain
  inline text link at the end reads more honestly than making the
  numbers themselves seem clickable. Now underlined (round 5) rather
  than color-differentiated, so the link doesn't rely on color alone
  to read as interactive (WCAG 1.4.1) now that it shares its color
  with the surrounding description text.

  PROPS:
    intro?  — small label above all strips; doubles as this section's
              <h2> (same pattern as round 2's landmark fix) — omit only
              if the copy genuinely has no intro line.
    strips  — array of case-study blocks:
                heading — case-study label (e.g. "A family
                          fuel-distribution business")
                subline — one line of context under the heading
                stats   — 2-4 { value, description } pairs
                href       — link target (case study or product page)
                linkLabel  — visible link text (e.g. "Read the full story")
                tone?      — "brand" | "gold" | "slate" | "muted",
                             which panel color this strip gets.
                             Defaults to "muted" if omitted.
    note?   — optional small closing line under all strips.
    id?     — optional anchor id.
*/
interface Stat {
  value:       string;
  description: string;
}

type Tone = "brand" | "gold" | "slate" | "muted";

interface Strip {
  heading:   string;
  subline:   string;
  stats:     Stat[];
  href:      string;
  linkLabel: string;
  tone?:     Tone;
}

interface Props {
  intro?: string;
  strips: Strip[];
  note?:  string;
  id?:    string;
}

const { intro, strips, note, id } = Astro.props;

// Grid shape depends on stat count so 3-stat strips never stretch
// across a 4-column layout with an empty cell.
function statGridClass(count: number): string {
  return count >= 4
    ? "grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-5 md:grid-cols-4"
    : "grid-cols-1 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-x-4 sm:gap-y-0";
}

// Panel background per tone (global.css). Text/bar/link no longer vary
// by tone as of round 5 — see the component doc comment above.
const TONE_PANEL_CLASS: Record<Tone, string> = {
  brand: "strip-panel strip-panel-brand",
  gold:  "strip-panel strip-panel-gold",
  slate: "strip-panel strip-panel-slate",
  muted: "strip-panel strip-panel-muted",
};
---

<section id={id} class="border-y border-border-subtle bg-surface-900 px-6 py-section-sm">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">

    <!-- Intro label — doubles as the section's <h2> landmark -->
    {intro && (
      <h2 class="reveal text-center text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-text-muted">
        {intro}
      </h2>
    )}

    <!-- ONE CONFIDENTLY-COLORED PANEL PER CASE STUDY (round 5) — wider gap than round 4 since bolder panels need more room to breathe. -->
    <div class:list={["flex flex-col gap-5", intro && "mt-8"]}>
      {strips.map((strip, i) => {
        const panelClass = TONE_PANEL_CLASS[strip.tone ?? "muted"];
        return (
          <article class:list={["reveal", panelClass]} style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 100}`}>

            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white sm:text-lg">
              {strip.heading}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-300">
              {strip.subline}
            </p>

            <!-- STAT GRID — round-1 stat-cell DNA, value size stepped down again, plain light divider bar -->
            <div class:list={["mt-5 grid", statGridClass(strip.stats.length)]}>
              {strip.stats.map((stat, j) => (
                <div
                  class="reveal border-s-2 border-white/25 ps-4 text-start"
                  style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 100 + j * 60}`}
                >
                  <p class="text-xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-2xl">
                    {stat.value}
                  </p>
                  <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                    {stat.description}
                  </p>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            <!--
              CLOSING LINK — plain inline text link, not a stretched card
              target. Underlined (round 5) instead of tone-colored, since
              it now shares its color with the description text — the
              underline (not color) is what signals "interactive" here.
              `py-1 -my-1` on the anchor widens the tap target a little on
              mobile without adding visible gap around the line (the
              negative margin cancels the padding's layout effect).
            -->
            <p class="mt-5 text-sm">
              <a
                href={strip.href}
                class="focus-ring focus-ring-on-900 group -my-1 inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-sm py-1 font-medium text-slate-300 underline decoration-slate-300/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/70"
              >
                {strip.linkLabel}
                <svg class="h-4 w-4 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 rtl:group-hover:-translate-x-1" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
                </svg>
              </a>
            </p>

          </article>
        );
      })}
    </div>

    {note && (
      <p class="reveal mt-8 text-center text-xs text-text-muted">
        {note}
      </p>
    )}

  </div>
</section>
